\f0\fs24 \cf0 US ACCIDENTS DATASET - PROJECT OVERVIEW\
\
Student: Hari Acharya & Santoshi Subedi Acharya\
\
Course: Data Analytics and Engineering Applications\
Date: December 15, 2024\
\
=================================================================\
DATASET INFORMATION\
=================================================================\
\
Source: Kaggle - US Accidents (2016-2023)\
URL: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/sobhanmoosavi/us-accidents\
Citation: Moosavi, Sobhan et al. (2019)\
\
Size: \
- Total Records: 7.7 million accidents\
- Sample Used: 500,000 records\
- Geographic Coverage: 49 US states\
- Time Period: February 2016 - March 2023\
\
Key Features (46 attributes):\
- Severity: 1-4 scale (TARGET VARIABLE)\
- Location: Latitude, Longitude, State, City\
- Time: Start_Time, Hour, Day of Week, Month\
- Weather: Temperature, Visibility, Precipitation, Wind Speed\
- Road: Junction, Traffic_Signal, Crossing, Stop, Bump\
- Points of Interest: Amenity, Station, Railway\
\
=================================================================\
WHY THIS DATASET IS APPROPRIATE\
=================================================================\
\
1. Real-world Impact:\
   - Addresses critical public safety issue\
   - 1.19 million traffic deaths globally per year\
   - Practical applications for emergency services\
\
2. Demonstrates Course Concepts:\
   - Large-scale data analysis (500K+ records)\
   - Data preprocessing (missing values, outliers)\
   - Feature engineering (15+ derived features)\
   - Clustering analysis (K-Means, DBSCAN)\
   - Classification (KNN, Random Forest, Ensemble)\
   - Model evaluation and comparison\
\
3. Dataset Quality:\
   - Well-documented and peer-reviewed\
   - Published in academic conferences\
   - Widely used in research (200+ citations)\
   - Clean and structured format\
   - Sufficient size for statistical significance\
\
4. Suitable Complexity:\
   - Multiple features for analysis\
   - Balanced between simplicity and complexity\
   - Allows for comprehensive ML pipeline\
   - Geographic and temporal dimensions\
\
=================================================================\
PROJECT SCOPE\
=================================================================\
\
Primary Goals:\
1. Predict accident severity (1-4) using classification\
2. Identify geographic hotspots using clustering\
3. Analyze temporal and weather patterns\
\
Course Concepts Applied:\
- Lectures 5-10: Data preprocessing\
- Lecture 26: Feature engineering & selection\
- Lectures 11-15: Clustering (K-Means, DBSCAN)\
- Lecture 20: KNN classification\
- Lectures 23-25: Ensemble learning (Random Forest, Stacking)\
\
Expected Deliverables:\
- 8-slide presentation\
- 14+ visualizations\
- 4 trained ML models\
- Comprehensive analysis report\
\
=================================================================\
PRELIMINARY RESULTS\
=================================================================\
\
Model Performance:\
- Random Forest: 85.2% accuracy (BEST)\
- Stacking Ensemble: 84.8% accuracy\
- KNN: 78.3% accuracy\
- Decision Tree: 80.1% accuracy\
\
Key Findings:\
- Identified 10 major accident hotspot clusters\
- Time of day and weather are strongest predictors\
- Rush hours (7-9 AM, 4-7 PM) show highest accident rates\
- California, Texas, Florida have highest concentrations\
\
